  "worked_solution": "First, substituting\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=4Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get the cone whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis:\n\n$$\n{Z}^{2}={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn cylindrical polar coordinates, this cone has the equation\n\n$$\nZ=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8Z,\\,3X,\\,4Y\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,4r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8r,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta \u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $4r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
